# Infectiousness is about a value REACHING an operation, not about a value
# holding one. A list built from two failures is a list of two failures: it
# has a length, it can be indexed, and rendering it shows what it holds.
#
# Native disagreed, and only native. `k_render` opens with
#
#     if (v.tag == K_ERR) return v;
#
# which fires for an err ANYWHERE it is reached, including an element of the
# list being rendered — so printing the list handed the merged failure to the
# endpoint and the successes went with it. Twenty lines further down the same
# function has `case K_ERR: return k_concat(k_str("err "), ...)`, which is the
# oracle's answer and was dead code: the early return caught every err first.
# Two places deciding one thing, and the one that ran was not the one that was
# right.
#
# The interpreter and wasm both answer through `eval::render`, which has only
# the nested case, so this is native alone.
fn boom what
  err what

listed = [(boom "a") (boom "b") 3]

pub play =
  print "length: {length listed}"
  >> print "listed: {listed}"
